**Person-row errors -- NEW persons 400 "birth month after start" message
(THIS attempt's change).** Edited Darrell Gallion's existing birth-month
input (type=month) to 2099-01 (after start_date 2026-09) via a real
htmx-triggered `change` event (the form's own `hx-trigger="change
delay:500ms..."`, not a hand-built request) -- confirmed a genuine
POST /whatif/settings 400 round-trip in the network log. Result, both
themes, identical:
  - alertCount = 1 (announced once, no duplicate/stacked alert; replacing
    the prior refusal alert that had been showing on the same host).
  - Placement: first child of the settings `<form>` -- next to the form.
  - Text (read via .textContent, i.e. what AT actually exposes): "Darrell
    Gallion's birth month (2099-01) is after the plan's start date
    (2026-09)" -- names the person, states the true reason, never says
    "needs a birth month" (the false claim checker-second's attempt-2 FAIL
    caught). Confirmed correctly classified for the DISTINCT "missing"
    shape too: a separately-posted request with an EMPTY birth month still
    returns 400 "Each person needs a birth month" -- the two classes remain
    properly disjoint post-fix.
  - Entity handling ("no raw codes or escaped entities like &#39;"):
    the server's raw response text over the wire IS entity-encoded
    (`Darrell Gallion&#39;s birth month ... plan&#39;s start date`,
    confirmed via a direct fetch() of the same endpoint) -- but this is
    correct HTML source, and it is only ever inserted via `innerHTML`-style
    parsing (base.js's buildErrorNode: `wrap.innerHTML = text`, unchanged
    infrastructure from WS4, confirmed unmodified by this diff). The LIVE
    DOM textContent after that parse contains real apostrophe characters,
    not literal "&#39;" text -- confirmed both by the JS read above and
    visually via screenshot (screenshot_light/dark_birthmonth_error.png):
    "Darrell Gallion's birth month (2099-01) is after the plan's start
    date (2026-09)" renders as ordinary legible prose in both themes, no
    entity garbage visible. A screen reader gets the same real apostrophe
    a sighted user sees. PASS.
  - HTML-injection/escaping sanity check (a person name containing
    `Rock & <b>Roll</b>`): server output is `Rock &amp; &lt;b&gt;Roll&lt;/
    b&gt;&#39;s birth month...` -- correctly escaped, renders as inert text
    (not a bold element), confirming the same innerHTML-safe path holds for
    adversarial names, not just this fixture's plain ones.
  - Focus: stays on the birth-month input the user was editing
    (document.activeElement = #person-birth-month-<id>) -- not lost, and
    point 5's "focus moves to... OR an aria-live region announces" is
    satisfied via role="alert" regardless (same mechanism as the refusal
